= Traffic flow theory {#ch:flow}

== Conservation of vehicles

Macroscopic models describe traffic as a fluid with density \m{k(x,t)} (vehicles per
kilometre), flow \m{q(x,t)} (vehicles per hour) and speed \m{v = q/k}. Vehicles are
neither created nor destroyed between junctions, which gives the conservation law of
\textcite{lighthill1955} and \textcite{richards1956}:

$$ \frac{\partial k}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial q}{\partial x} = 0 $$ {#eq:conservation}

On its own @eq:conservation has two unknowns. The LWR model\ix{LWR model} closes it by
assuming that flow is a function of density alone, \m{q = Q(k)}: the *fundamental
diagram*\ix{fundamental diagram} (@fig:fd).

\figure[id=fig:fd, width=11cm]{
  \image[alt="Flow against density: a parabola with its maximum at the capacity"]{../img/fundamental-diagram.svg}
  \caption{The Greenshields fundamental diagram with a free-flow speed of 100 km/h}
}

== The fundamental diagram

\textcite{greenshields1935} proposed a linear relation between speed and density, which
makes the fundamental diagram a parabola:

$$ Q(k) = v_f\, k \left(1 - \frac{k}{k_j}\right) $$ {#eq:greenshields}

where \m{v_f} is the free-flow speed and \m{k_j} the jam density\ix{jam density}. Its
maximum, the capacity\ix{capacity}, lies at \m{k_c = k_j/2}:

$$ q_{\max} = Q(k_c) = \frac{v_f\, k_j}{4} $$ {#eq:capacity}

Later work favours a triangular diagram, which fits measured data better at high
densities \cite{daganzo1997, treiber2013}. @tab:fd lists the parameters used in this
thesis.

\caption[Table, id=tab:fd]{Fundamental diagram parameters for the study site}
\table[style="Thesis Table", header-rows=1, widths=(6cm, 2.5cm, 2.5cm), columns=(left, decimal, left)]{
  | Parameter          | Value | Unit    |
  | Free-flow speed    | 100   | km/h    |
  | Jam density        | 150   | veh/km  |
  | Capacity per lane  | 2100  | veh/h   |
  | Wave speed         | 18.5  | km/h    |
}

== The cell transmission model

\textcite{daganzo1994} discretises @eq:conservation into cells of length \m{\Delta x} and
time steps \m{\Delta t}. The flow between cells \m{i} and \m{i+1} is the smaller of what
cell \m{i} can send and what cell \m{i+1} can receive:

$$ q_{i+1/2} = \min\left\{ S(k_i),\ R(k_{i+1}) \right\} $$ {#eq:ctm-flow}

and the densities are updated with

$$ k_i^{t+1} = k_i^t + \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta x}\left(q_{i-1/2} - q_{i+1/2}\right) $$ {#eq:ctm-update}

The model is stable when a vehicle at free-flow speed cannot cross a whole cell in one
time step, \m{v_f\, \Delta t \leq \Delta x}: the CFL condition\ix{CFL condition}.
